(Secure Face Registration and Verification Framework) Tj
T*
/F1 12 Tf
16 TL
(using Face Quality Assessment and Liveness Detection) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
14 TL
(Final Project Report -- Nishika) Tj
T*
(Report Date: August 6, 2026) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 14 Tf
18 TL
(1. The Problem) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
13 TL
(Think of this project as building the brain of a security guard for face-based login.) Tj
T*
(The guard has two jobs. First, when someone signs up \(registration\), take a clear photo) Tj
T*
(of their face and remember it. Second, when someone tries to log in later) Tj
T*
(\(verification\), look at their face again and answer two questions: is this a real, live) Tj
T*
(person in front of the camera right now, and does this face actually belong to the) Tj
T*
(person who signed up.) Tj
T*
T*
(The reason this is hard is that a face-matching system alone can be tricked. Someone) Tj
T*
(could hold up a printed photo, play a video, or display a photo on a phone screen, and a) Tj
T*
(system that only checks whether a face matches might be fooled. So the system also) Tj
T*
(needs a way to catch these tricks, called liveness detection, and a way to make sure the) Tj
T*
(photo it is working with is actually usable in the first place, called image quality) Tj
T*
(assessment.) Tj
T*
T*
(The project brief splits this into two phases, verification and registration. This design) Tj
T*
(merges them into one shared pipeline with two entry points, since both phases reuse the) Tj
T*
(same quality, liveness, and matching logic. The full pipeline is shown in Figure 1,) Tj
T*
(Section 2.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
